WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:12.129
independent journalism.

00:00:12.129 --> 00:00:14.129
Uh, so great to talk to you, Richard.

00:00:14.129 --> 00:00:16.209
Really appreciate you for having me.

00:00:16.209 --> 00:00:21.380
So listen, we've been talking at the federal
level about what I've been seeing as red flags

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:26.340
when it comes to press freedom and this, a
lot of it has been coming from the Oval Office.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:32.770
It includes everything from of making it easier
to sue media outlets, journalism licenses,

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:37.810
a refusal of interview or even spots in the
press room at the White House based on the

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:42.350
sort of a perceived a friendliness to the
administration.

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:47.540
But it's not just at the federal level that
there are things happening when it comes to

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:48.540
press freedom.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.820
A lot of stuff, as in many other areas seems
to be happening at the state level.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:52.930
Is that right?

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:58.740
I would argue that that's probably true too,
although there's probably less chance for

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.890
traction at the state level.

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:05.510
Um, although you can somebody throw up a lot
of impediments.

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:08.810
Let's talk a, or, yeah, well, I was going
to say the ethics in Journalism Act specifically

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:14.670
in Georgia is something that many people probably
have not heard of, but which, which would

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.950
have a significant impact on, on journalists
work.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:24.200
Well, this was a bill dropped right at the
end of the legislative session.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:29.510
It became public on the 2nd of April, which
was the last day, and it was filed on the

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:30.510
first night.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:38.970
I thought it was an April fools joke initially,
but what it proposed was a journalism oversight

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:46.630
board, a board, um, appointed initially by
the Chancellor of the University of Georgia,

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:54.250
the board to be based at the very prestigious
Grady College of journalism at a Uga, uh,

00:01:54.250 --> 00:02:02.430
to be made up of three editors, three news
producers to web, uh, journalists and one

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:05.360
retired journalism professor.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:13.390
And the bill's suggests that the, uh, folks,
they're, the nine folks would create a candidate

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:20.740
of standards for journalism, um, and, uh,
credit journalists within the state and in

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:26.189
forced the professional standards, uh, for
journalism in the state.

00:02:26.189 --> 00:02:34.499
Um, the bill would also require that all outtakes
from interviews such as this one, um, would

00:02:34.499 --> 00:02:41.829
have to be provided to the interviewee within
three days of a request.

00:02:41.829 --> 00:02:48.560
So, um, uh, outtakes, interview notes, all
photographs.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:53.569
It's sort of an harassment measure against
existing journalism.

00:02:53.569 --> 00:03:00.870
And, and in a not so subtle dig at journalists,
the bill would require that the journalists

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:07.879
turn over the outtakes and photographs within
three days, which is precisely the same period

00:03:07.879 --> 00:03:13.349
that government in Georgia has to respond
to the open records act.

00:03:13.349 --> 00:03:17.999
Um, there's no way, thankfully, that this
bill will pass.

00:03:17.999 --> 00:03:20.269
At least I don't think it can pass.

00:03:20.269 --> 00:03:30.480
And even if it did pass, um, the governor
of Georgia has said that he won't sign it

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.349
and I doubt there would be enough votes to,
uh, sustain it.

00:03:35.349 --> 00:03:44.269
But it does present a really interesting insight
into what I describe as the Zeit geist now

00:03:44.269 --> 00:03:45.730
in the country.

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:55.329
Now, this bill was proposed by six really
well educated people who are respected in

00:03:55.329 --> 00:03:56.329
the legislature.

00:03:56.329 --> 00:04:01.359
That's a former missionary, uh, who's also
works as a contractor.

00:04:01.359 --> 00:04:07.900
There's a pharmacist, there's a doctor, a
lawyer, a retired county agent who's chair

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:13.489
of the education committee, and there's a
real estate development developer behind this.

00:04:13.489 --> 00:04:15.480
They're all Republican.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:23.220
Uh, there as I said, well thought of, they
come from predominantly rural areas and 10

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:28.650
years ago they would have been the laughing
stock for pushing a bill like this.

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:37.139
Now, I think the tone has changed so much
such that there's such, um, on the republican

00:04:37.139 --> 00:04:43.509
side, such disdain for mainstream media, uh,
that they have a pass to do it.

00:04:43.509 --> 00:04:47.650
And probably when they went back to their
districts, they got slapped on the backs and

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:48.900
Atta Boys.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:52.060
Um, and that, that is a really disturbing
trend.

00:04:52.060 --> 00:05:00.180
If you, if you look at the latest Edelman
Press Trust survey that came out in January,

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:05.729
it's a global survey with an amazing 0.6 margin
of error.

00:05:05.729 --> 00:05:18.879
And in the US, um, 69% of Democrats said to
trust the media, but only 23% of Republicans.

00:05:18.879 --> 00:05:22.810
Now that is a startling disconnect.

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:28.909
And that's the thing that scares me the most
here and really is why bills like this can

00:05:28.909 --> 00:05:31.130
come up and get traction.

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:32.130
Yeah.

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:36.150
I mean there's this, earlier in the program
we talked about this in regard to the debate

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:41.440
that is going on as to whether children who
are being kept in what are effectively large

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:47.509
cages at the border deserve soap and bedding,
where there is a creeping normalization.

00:05:47.509 --> 00:05:52.440
If 20 years ago you'd try to jump right to
that part of the debate.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:54.120
You get no traction whatsoever.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:06:00.909
But over time as the sort of Overton window
is pushed a little more and more in a particular

00:06:00.909 --> 00:06:05.789
direction, then all of a sudden it seems at
least in some circles reasonable to debate

00:06:05.789 --> 00:06:08.500
something like the accreditation of journalists.

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:13.690
Well, I think, um, this is not just a US issue.

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:21.081
Uh, I do a lot of work in eastern Europe,
uh, helping news organizations there, deal

00:06:21.081 --> 00:06:26.069
with, uh, threats against their operations.

00:06:26.069 --> 00:06:28.560
And particularly in Poland.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:34.150
Uh, you see, uh, efforts there to control
the press.

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:39.910
And there it started with adjustments into
the constitution of the chords and who could

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:40.919
be a judge.

00:06:40.919 --> 00:06:44.169
And how long one could stay or judge.

00:06:44.169 --> 00:06:48.610
So existing judges, you had lots of experiences
were removed and then the judges who were

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:52.960
put in were much more hostile to a free press.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:07:00.030
And they're the independent journalists, um,
are under assault, um, for their own issue

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:05.610
because some of them are American homes, some
of them are, have German partial German ownership.

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:14.650
And so the attacks come as a sort of a licensing
component, but it is all about, um, the threat

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:18.879
to the government that independent journalism
presents.

00:07:18.879 --> 00:07:27.180
And when the, uh, government in question is
hanging on by a thread, as is the case in

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:30.439
Poland, they have a very narrow majority.

00:07:30.439 --> 00:07:36.539
Uh, so the efforts to constrain the press
become more vigorous.

00:07:36.539 --> 00:07:42.229
Now, I don't want to draw too many comparisons
to Georgia, but Georgia for the first time

00:07:42.229 --> 00:07:46.499
is a state in play.

00:07:46.499 --> 00:07:51.210
And I suspect that also comes as part of it.

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:59.060
Here you have folks who are really wanting
to pump up their base and wanting to get folks

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:02.569
really enthusiastic and out of the Poles.

00:08:02.569 --> 00:08:11.180
And the, uh, media frankly is, uh, the boogeyman
for a lot of Republicans.

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:17.909
Uh, and it's easy to charge people up and,
and get people fired up by attacking a free

00:08:17.909 --> 00:08:19.590
and independent press.

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:20.590
Yeah.

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:27.219
It's been incredibly successful to use this
a fake news term to basically become a catchall

00:08:27.219 --> 00:08:32.380
for anything that contradicts the preexisting
narrative that may exist in some political

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:35.430
circles or that is critical of the current
administration.

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:40.370
And what's most disturbing to me is not that
this is something that the current administration

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:44.220
has attempted, but the success that has come
with it.

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:48.170
I mean, you know, the, the number of tweets
and youtube comments that I get daily saying

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:53.000
that my program is fake news is probably not
altogether surprising because whatever is

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:57.260
kind of in, in the, the insult of the day
I am the recipient of.

00:08:57.260 --> 00:09:01.160
But the serious way in which as you point
out, it has affected particularly Republican

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:06.750
voters into believing that at some fundamental
level, basic news reporting cannot be trusted.

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:10.280
That partisan divide is what shows me how
effective it's been.

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:16.360
Well, I think there's another thing that journalists
like us, how to look at each other and in

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:21.850
the face and say we have allowed ourselves
to become too siloed.

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:29.220
Um, and, uh, part of the breakdown in trust
is that we have not necessarily have been

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:34.380
as transparent as we should be about our processes.

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:35.970
Uh, there's a

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:37.880
in what, in what sense do you mean?

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:45.020
Well, I think the, the public often look at
institute large journalism institutions and

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:47.640
they don't know how the magic happens.

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:55.030
They don't know the amount of work that goes
into reporting out a new story, how, how many

00:09:55.030 --> 00:10:02.700
clothes got made, how many, uh, documents
were examined, how many foyer requests were

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:09.400
made, how many efforts were made to get somebody
who didn't speak to speak.

00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:13.940
And we're not transparent enough, uh, about
that process.

00:10:13.940 --> 00:10:20.320
There's a terrific initiative coming out to
the University of Georgia and pointer, uh,

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:28.320
the trusting news initiative where they're
actively working to coach news organizations

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:36.530
to push openness and transparency even goes
so far in some newsrooms to open up their

00:10:36.530 --> 00:10:41.890
editorial meetings, two Facebook lives so
people can see how those editorial discussions

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:42.890
go.

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:46.050
How is the newspaper going to be stacked tomorrow?

00:10:46.050 --> 00:10:47.620
What's going to be the lead story?

00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:51.150
What stories are they're choosing and why
are they choosing those stories?

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:58.700
Um, at, at CNN when I was there, there was
a lot of action on our part towards explaining

00:10:58.700 --> 00:11:04.980
why we did stories and how we did them and
what the process was for reporting out those

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:05.980
stories.

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:09.990
Those are all the kinds of things that help
build trust.

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:15.420
But as I say earlier, uh, that it's one step.

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:18.290
It's not a, the total solution.

00:11:18.290 --> 00:11:24.310
We've, we've got to figure out other ways
of winning back that trust.

00:11:24.310 --> 00:11:26.380
I agree 100%.

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:29.870
I think the other side of this, and I'd be
curious to hear your thought about it in the

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:33.840
limited time we have is the lack of media
literacy that we increasingly have in this

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:34.840
country.

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:39.190
I, uh, recently adjunct taught a course on
critical thinking and new media at Boston

00:11:39.190 --> 00:11:40.190
College.

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:47.080
And a huge component of it was just explaining
to students what the differences are between

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:52.820
something like fake news and something like
simply that there is editorial bias in a story

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:57.310
and being able to distinguish between news
and opinion journalism, understanding that

00:11:57.310 --> 00:12:01.630
anonymous source doesn't necessarily mean
anonymous to the journalist, but it is a,

00:12:01.630 --> 00:12:06.050
a sources identity that may be kept private
from the public perspective.

00:12:06.050 --> 00:12:10.950
All of these basic things to me seem to be
completely missing from education right now.

00:12:10.950 --> 00:12:13.050
I completely agree with that.

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:20.040
Um, I uh, teach, uh, the University of North
Carolina, uh, each year and, uh, there's an

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:27.500
intro course that is big survey course taught
by the Dean that I help, uh, with.

00:12:27.500 --> 00:12:34.210
And those kids are everything from premeds
to future journalism major to chemistry majors.

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:44.040
You have to feel fulfilled some, uh, preliminary
requirements and, uh, they're very startled

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:45.130
to hear that.

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:48.740
What journalists actually do know the names
of, of sources.

00:12:48.740 --> 00:12:51.890
Um, uh, precisely at the point.

00:12:51.890 --> 00:12:57.140
But also, you know, we as journalists need
to be much more careful about how we identify.

00:12:57.140 --> 00:13:02.740
Our guest is the person who's on the air,
a reporter delivering a report.

00:13:02.740 --> 00:13:03.740
Yes.

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:09.870
Or like me, uh, somebody who's giving their
opinion on, on, on journalism based on years

00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:11.270
of experience.

00:13:11.270 --> 00:13:16.120
So I'm, I'm certainly, uh, an an opinion person.

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:20.330
I'm not a reporter, although I played that
role in my career.

00:13:20.330 --> 00:13:27.800
So identifying, uh, who we have on the air,
who the people are in the stories providing

00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:30.820
their expertise is absolutely critical.

00:13:30.820 --> 00:13:31.820
Yes.

00:13:31.820 --> 00:13:35.510
Well, on that note, Richard Griffiths is president
of the Georgia First Amendment Foundation

00:13:35.510 --> 00:13:41.280
and he has been here giving us his opinion
on the basis that he has informed that opinion,

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:44.060
uh, personally and not here as a reporter.

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:45.910
Richard, really appreciate your time today
on this really important issue.

00:13:45.910 --> 00:13:46.180
Thank you for having me on

